# 000000-000099/20040709T211904Z_4534600.wiki
zΔ⚡b|=205|-2|=1|+irs|=46|-5|+second|=11d|+%0A|=2|+ History %3D%3D%0A%0AThe '''diff''' program was developed in the early 1970s on the [[Unix]] operating system which was emerging from [[ATT%7CAT&T]] [[Bell Labs]] in Murray Hill, New Jersey.%0AThe final version was written completely by [[Douglas McIlroy]].%0AThis research was published in a 1976 paper which he co%2Dwrote with James W. Hunt who wrote one of the initial prototypes of diff.%0A%0AMcIlroy's work was preceded and influenced by [[Steve Johnson]]'s comparison program on [[GECOS]] and [[Mike Lesk]]'s '''proof''' program developed on Unix.%0A'''Proof''' produced line%2Dby%2Dline differential comparisons like diff and even used angle%2Dbrackets (">" and "<") for presenting line insertions and deletions for output.%0AThe heuristics they used were deemed unreliable, and the usefulness of diff invoked McIlroy to research and design a more robust tool that could be used in a variety of tasks but perform well given the processing and space limitations of the [[PDP%2D11]] hardware.%0A%0AIn the context of Unix, the use of [[ed]] provided diff with the natural ability to create machine%2Dusable "edit scripts".%0AThese edit scripts could then be saved to a file which along with the original file allow '''ed''' to produce the modified file in its entirety.%0AThis realization greatly reduced the space necessary to maintain multiple versions of a file.%0AMcIlroy considered writing a post%2Dprocessor for diff where a variety of output formats could be designed and implemented, but he found it more frugal and simpler to have diff generate the syntax and reverse%2Dorder targeted for '''ed'''.%0A%0AMost all diff implementations remain outwardly unchanged ever since 1975 with only the addition of improvements of the algorithm and any useful features and output formats.%0APostprocessors sdiff and diffmk provided side%2Dby%2Dside diff listings and applying change marks to printed documents, respectively, and were developed elsewhere in Bell Labs in or before 1981.%0AThe [[BSD%7CBerkeley distribution of Unix]] made a point of adding the context format (%2DC) and recursion on filesystem directory structures (%2Dr).%0AThe GNU System's diff application includes the unified context format and is combined in a package with other diff and [[patch]] related utilities.%0A%0AAt the time of diff's origin, the main uses were for making comparisons in computer language source code, the source to technical documents, program debugging output, filesystem listings and computer assembly code.%0AThe output targeted for '''ed''' was purposely added to allow compression of a sequence of modifications to a file.%0A[[SCCS%7CThe Source Code Control System]] (SCCS) which emerged in the late 1970s, was a direct consequence of this development.%0AThe context format of diff introduced at Berkeley helped in distributing patches to code that possibly may have been modified.%0APaul Jensen, faced with consolidating changes from two branches of a program's source code tree proposed diff3, which persists for most as a curiosity, but is still useful to those finding themselves in Jensen's situation.%0A%0A%3D%3D |=1d|+ |=17f|+ |=a|+ |=284|
