# 000000-000099/20050407T174840Z_12292124.wiki
zΔ⚡b|+In [[computing]], <code>'''diff'''</code> is a [[Unix]] utility that outputs the difference between two text files.%0A%3D%3DUsage%3D%3D%0AThe output of this program is also called a ''diff''. It is invoked from the command line with the name of two files:%0A%0A  $ diff firstone.txt secondone.txt%0A%0AThe result might look like this:%0A%0A 0a1,3%0A > This is an important notice! It should%0A > therefore be located at the beginning of%0A > this document!%0A 7,12d9%0A < This paragraph contains text that is%0A < outdated %2D it will be deprecated and%0A < deleted in the near future.%0A < This is an important notice! It should%0A < therefore be located at the beginning of%0A < this document!%0A 14,15c11,14%0A < spell check this dokument. On the other%0A < hand, I could do with some shoarma.%0A %2D%2D%2D%0A > spell check this document. On the other%0A > hand, I could do with some shoarma.%0A > This paragraph contains important new%0A > additions to this document.%0A%0ANote that in this ''normal diff output'', '''a''' stands for ''added'', '''d''' for ''deleted'' and '''c''' for ''changed''. By default, lines common to both files are not shown. Lines that have moved will show up as added on their new location and as deleted on their old location.%0A%0AIn ''unified format'', each line that occurs only in the first file is preceded by a minus sign, each line that occurs only in the second file is preceded by a plus sign, and common lines are preceded by a space.%0A%0ALines beginning with three plus signs indicate the number of lines in each ''hunk'', the file names, and where in the files to find them. Diff output is often used as input to the [[patch (Unix)%7Cpatch]] program.%0A%0AThe first editions of the diff program were designed for line comparisons in text files.%0ABy the 1980s, support for binary files was necessary resulting in a shift in the application's design and implementation.%0A%0A%3D%3D History %3D%3D%0A%0AThe '''diff''' program was developed in the early 1970s on the [[Unix]] operating system which was emerging from [[ATT%7CAT&T]] [[Bell Labs]] in Murray Hill, New Jersey.%0AThe final version incorporated into these early Unix systems was written completely by [[Douglas McIlroy]].%0AThis research was published in a 1976 paper which he co%2Dwrote with [[James W. Hunt]] who also wrote one of the initial prototypes of diff.%0A%0AMcIlroy's work was preceded and influenced by [[Stephen C. Johnson%7CSteve Johnson]]'s comparison program on [[GECOS]] and [[Mike Lesk]]'s '''proof''' program%2D%2Dwhich like diff%2D%2Dalso originated on Unix.%0A'''Proof''' produced line%2Dby%2Dline changes like diff and even used angle%2Dbrackets (">" and "<") for presenting line insertions and deletions in the program's output.%0AThe [[heuristic]]s these applications used were deemed unreliable though.%0AThe potential usefulness of a diff tool provoked McIlroy into researching and designing a more robust tool that could be used in a variety of tasks but perform well in the processing and space limitations of the [[PDP%2D11]]'s hardware. His approach was also a result from collaboration with individuals at Bell Labs including [[Alfred Aho]], [[Elliot Pinson]], [[Jeffrey Ullman]], and [[Harold S. Stone]].%0A%0AIn the context of Unix, the use of the [[ed]] line editor provided diff with the natural ability to create machine%2Dusable "edit scripts".%0AThese edit scripts when saved to a file can, along with the original file, be reconsitituted by '''ed''' into the modified file in its entirety.%0AThis greatly reduced the space necessary to maintain multiple versions of a file.%0AMcIlroy considered writing a post%2Dprocessor for diff where a variety of output formats could be designed and implemented, but he found it more frugal and simpler to have diff be responsible for generating the syntax and reverse%2Dorder input accepted by the '''ed''' command.%0A%0AIn diff's early years, common uses included comparing changes in programming language source code, source to technical documents, verifying program debugging output, comparing filesystem listings and analyzing computer assembly code.%0AThe output targeted for '''ed''' was motivated to provide compression for a sequence of modifications made to a file.%0AThe [[SCCS%7CSource Code Control System]] (SCCS) emerged in the late 1970s as a direct consequence of this development.%0A%0AA conceptual predecessor of diff includes [[Project Xanadu]], a hypertext project established in 1960 that had envisioned a version tracking system necessary for its "transpointing windows" feature.%0AAs part of this feature, file differences were subsumed in the expansive term "[[transclusion]]", when a document has included in it parts of other documents or revisions.%0A%0AIn the digital realm of the humanities, computer comparison systems were understood to have been created for working on literary works published in large volumes.%0A%0A%3D%3D%3DVariations%3D%3D%3D%0A%0AMost all diff implementations remain outwardly unchanged since 1975.%0AThe modifications include improvements to the core algorithm, the addition of useful features to the command, and the design of new output formats.%0A%0APostprocessors '''sdiff''' and '''diffmk''' render side%2Dby%2Dside diff listings and applied change marks to printed documents, respectively.%0ABoth were developed elsewhere in Bell Labs in or before 1981.%0A%0AThe [[BSD%7CBerkeley distribution of Unix]] made a point of adding the context format (%2DC) and the ability to recurse on filesystem directory structures (%2Dr).%0A%0AThe context format of diff introduced at Berkeley helped with distributing patches for source code that may have been changed minimally.%0A%0A[[Paul Jensen]], faced with consolidating changes from two branches of a program's source code tree proposed [[diff3]], which persists for most as a curiosity, but is still useful to those finding themselves in Jensen's situation.%0A%0AThe [[GNU%7CGNU Project]]'s diff application includes the unified context format and is combined in a package with other diff and [[patch]] related utilities.%0A%0A[[Image:WinDiff_demonstration.png%7Cthumb%7Cright%7CWinDiff sample output]][[Microsoft]]'s '''WinDiff''' is a graphical diff%2Dlike program. Although criticized for it's user interface and lack of documentation, it can still be a useful tool. It's limited file selection options have inspired a front%2Dend called [http://www.codeproject.com/tools/runwindiff.asp RunWinDiff].%0A%0A%3D%3D Free Software Implementations %3D%3D%0A%0AThe [[GNU]] Project has an implementation of diff (and diff3) that is available from the [http://www.gnu.org/software/diffutils/diffutils.html GNU diffutils] package.%0A%0ASeveral tools on various platforms use the GNU diffutils engine and provide a graphical display, and some combine editing and merging capabilities. The following are some of these [[free software%7Cfree]] tools.%0A%0A* [[Emacs]] %2D Provided by Ediff mode.%0A* [http://home.catv.ne.jp/pp/ginoue/software/gtkdiff/index%2De.html gtkdiff]%0A* [http://kdiff3.sourceforge.net KDiff3]%0A* [[kompare]]%0A* [http://meld.sourceforge.net Meld]%0A* [http://tkdiff.sourceforge.net tkdiff]%0A* [http://winmerge.sourceforge.net WinMerge]%0A* [http://xxdiff.sourceforge.net xxdiff]%0A%0A%3D%3D See also %3D%3D%0A* [[Levenshtein distance]]%0A* [[Longest%2Dcommon subsequence problem]]%0A* [[Patch (Unix)%7CPatch]]%0A* [[Revision Control System]]%0A* [[Source Code Control System]]%0A%0A%3D%3D References %3D%3D%0A* Hunt, James W. and McIlroy, M. Douglas, "An Algorithm for Differential File Comparison," 41, Computing Science Technical Report, Bell Laboratories, June 1976. [http://www.cs.dartmouth.edu/~doug/]%0A%0A* "Comparing and Merging Files with GNU Diff and Patch", by David MacKenzie, Paul Eggert, and Richard Stallman (ISBN 0954161750) [http://www.gnu.org/software/diffutils/manual/]%0A%0A* E. Myers, "An O(ND) Difference Algorithm and Its Variations," Algorithmica 1, 2 (1986), 251%2D266. [http://www.cs.arizona.edu/people/gene/PAPERS/diff.ps (Postscript)]%0A%0A* A generic implementation of the Myers SES/LCS algorithm with the Hirschberg linear space refinement [http://www.ioplex.com/~miallen/libmba/dl/src/diff.c (C source code)]%0A%0A%3D%3DExternal links%3D%3D%0A* [http://www.gnu.org/software/diffutils/diffutils.html GNU Diff utilities]. Made available by the [[Free Software Foundation]]. Free Documentation. Free source code.%0A%0A[[de:Diff]]%0A%0A[[Category:Unix programs]]|
